# Git Workflow

Standardize git operations for the monorepo (Python backend + Next.js frontend).

## Branch Naming

Use prefixes to indicate branch purpose:

| Prefix | Purpose | Example |
|--------|---------|---------|
| `feature/` | New functionality | `feature/signal-analysis-pipeline` |
| `fix/` | Bug fixes | `fix/scraping-timeout-retry` |
| `refactor/` | Code restructuring | `refactor/llm-provider-abstract` |
| `docs/` | Documentation only | `docs/api-endpoint-guide` |
| `test/` | Test additions | `test/article-generation` |
| `chore/` | Build, CI, deps | `chore/upgrade-next-16` |

Keep branch names lowercase with hyphens. Max 50 characters.

## Commit Messages

Follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<scope>): <short summary>

<optional body - explain WHY, not WHAT>
```

### Types
- `feat` — new feature
- `fix` — bug fix
- `refactor` — code change that neither fixes nor adds
- `test` — adding/fixing tests
- `docs` — documentation only
- `chore` — build, CI, dependency updates
- `perf` — performance improvement

### Scopes
Use the area of the codebase: `backend`, `frontend`, `scraping`, `llm`, `api`, `ci`, `deps`.

### Examples
```
feat(scraping): add retry with exponential backoff

fix(api): return 404 for unknown signal IDs

refactor(llm): extract provider interface from OpenAI impl

test(frontend): add snapshot tests for SignalCard
```

## Pull Requests

### Requirements
- Descriptive title following conventional commit format
- Summary of changes and *why* they were made
- All CI checks passing (lint, tests, type checks)
- At least one review approval
- No merge conflicts with base branch

### PR Size
- Prefer small, focused PRs (< 400 lines of changes)
- Large features should be split into multiple PRs
- If a PR is large, explain the breakdown in the description

### Description Template
```markdown
## Summary
What changed and why.

## Test Plan
- [ ] Unit tests pass
- [ ] Manual testing done (describe)
- [ ] No regressions in related areas

## Screenshots
(if UI changes)
```

## Merge Strategy

- **Squash merge** for feature branches — keeps history clean
- **Merge commit** only for long-lived branches (e.g., `release/*`)
- Delete branch after merge

## Tags & Releases

- Tag releases as `v<MAJOR>.<MINOR>.<PATCH>` (semver)
- Backend and frontend can be versioned independently if needed: `backend-v1.2.0`, `frontend-v1.3.0`
- Always tag from `main` branch

## Protecting Main

- No direct pushes to `main`
- All changes via PR
- Require CI to pass before merge
- Require up-to-date branch before merge
